Looking at the `MQTT_PROP_TYPE_BINARY_DATA` case:
```c
case MQTT_PROP_TYPE_BINARY_DATA:
  if (i + 2 > end) return 0;
  prop->val.len = (uint16_t) ((((uint16_t) i[0]) << 8) | i[1]);
  prop->val.buf = (char *) i + 2;
  new_pos += 2 + prop->val.len;
  break;
```

The check is `if (i + 2 > end) return 0;` - this only validates that the 2-byte length field can be read. But it does NOT validate that `i + 2 + prop->val.len <= end` (i.e., the referenced payload extent). Compare with `MQTT_PROP_TYPE_STRING`:
```c
case MQTT_PROP_TYPE_STRING:
  if (i + 2 >= end) return 0;
  prop->val.len = (uint16_t) ((((uint16_t) i[0]) << 8) | i[1]);
  prop->val.buf = (char *) i + 2;
  if (i + 2 + prop->val.len > end) return 0;  // <-- this check exists for STRING
  new_pos += 2 + prop->val.len;
  break;
```

So for STRING, there's a check `if (i + 2 + prop->val.len > end) return 0;` but for BINARY_DATA, this check is missing. This means a binary-data property whose declared length exceeds the remaining packet will set `prop->val.buf` to point beyond the buffer, and `prop->val.len` will be larger than the actual remaining bytes. Later processing that consumes `prop->val.buf` for `prop->val.len` bytes will read beyond the input buffer.

